#!/usr/bin/env python3
# wiki-extension: Say what failed in a Jenkins job and why, and note in the wiki when it is fixed.
# wiki-events: tick
# wiki-setup: yes
# wiki-settings: yes
# wiki-detect: Jenkinsfile
"""wiki jenkins: an llm-wiki extension. Standard library only.

For agents that have no CI watching of their own. It asks Jenkins, over its JSON API, whether the latest
build of a job failed, and why: the first line of the log that looks like the error, and a short excerpt of
the end of the log, kept on this machine. Jenkins doesn't know which repository a job belongs to, so you
watch a clone together with the job that builds it. With a clone watched, the tick event leaves a line for
the next session on that branch when a build starts failing, and can save a note in the wiki when the
failure is fixed.

It only READS Jenkins, and it writes nothing anywhere: every request is a GET. It never starts, aborts or
rebuilds anything, never pushes, and starts no agent to fix anything. The API token is only ever sent to
the Jenkins address you set, and is never printed, logged, stored or put in an error.
"""
